+<h2>Advanced concepts</h2>
+<p class="note">The ideas this model actually leans on, each stated with its failure mode.</p>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Energy-based models</h3>
+<p>An RBM defines an energy for every visible-hidden configuration; learning lowers the energy of data and raises it elsewhere. The gradient needs the model's own expectation, which is intractable - that is the whole reason for contrastive divergence.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Why one Gibbs step is enough</h3>
+<p>CD-1 is biased but cheap: it does not follow the true gradient, yet it reliably moves parameters in a useful direction. The bias is why DBNs were displaced by backpropagation-based autoencoders, and why the repo's CD-1 loop matters for reproducing the numbers.</p>
+</div>
+<div class="card">
+<h3>Layerwise features as a ladder</h3>
+<p>Each layer's activations are a legitimate feature set, so you can stop early for a cheaper model. The platform treats the DBN as a feature provider, which is how it can serve an RUL regressor without retraining anything downstream.</p>
+</div>
